 \ReviewerComment{3. Please present more complete event-detection metrics for all rainfall thresholds and baseline models.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{We have expanded the event-detection reporting in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:event_detection}, Figure~\ref{manuscript-fig:r2m4_event_csi} and Supplementary Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_fixed_events}--\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_ensemble_events}. The fixed intensity thresholds are 0.1, 0.5 and 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$. The training-quantile thresholds are Q90 $=0.295784$, Q95 $=0.574048$ and Q97.5 $=0.932169$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (rounded to six decimal places), estimated from 18,404 windowed training targets rather than the test set. The six learning models are XGBoost, Transformer, BU-LSTM, BiLSTM-Attention, BU-LSTM-Attention and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention. The three references are Persistence, training-fitted AR(1), and training-fitted month-by-hour climatology.
+
+We report the available POD, FAR, CSI and ROC-AUC, together with forecast and hit counts. POD is $\mathrm{TP}/(\mathrm{TP}+\mathrm{FN})$, FAR is the false alarm ratio $\mathrm{FP}/(\mathrm{TP}+\mathrm{FP})$, and CSI is $\mathrm{TP}/(\mathrm{TP}+\mathrm{FP}+\mathrm{FN})$. Under the fixed rule, the predicted mean is compared with the observed-event intensity threshold ($c_\tau=\tau$). Under the validation-selected rule, each method's cutoff maximizes validation CSI over up to 300 distinct candidates and is then frozen for testing. ROC-AUC uses the continuous forecast score and is independent of that decision cutoff, while depending on the observed-event definition $y\geq\tau$. For the probabilistic models, these mean-based event decisions are distinct from the exceedance-probability Brier-score analysis.
+
+The reporting scope is explicit: Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_fixed_events} has six thresholds by nine methods, whereas Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_tuned_events} has five thresholds by nine methods. The validation-selected rule was not evaluated at 0.5~mm\,h$^{-1}$, and complete AUC results were not saved at that intensity. The unavailable fixed-rule AR(1)/climatology AUC entries remain dashes; undefined FAR when no events are forecast is also shown as a dash, not zero. We do not claim that every metric is available for every threshold, method and rule. The separate five-seed diagnostics in Tables~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_seed_scores} and~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_ensemble_events} cover only BiLSTM-Attention and BU-LSTM-Attention. Seed-level summaries, five-seed mean-prediction comparisons and the single saved runs are kept distinct.
+
+The adverse results are retained. Under the fixed rule, all 24 learning-model minus Persistence CSI differences at 0.5, Q95, Q97.5 and 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ are negative. At 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$, XGBoost detects only one observed event, while the other five learning models detect none. Validation-selected cutoffs recover detections, but the six learning models' FAR at 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ remains 0.859--0.916. At Q95 under that rule, Transformer CSI is 0.133, below Persistence's 0.156. ROC-AUC describes ranking information and does not establish useful warning performance. At some event thresholds, the climatology's selected cutoff lies at the lower boundary of its candidate grid, so that comparison can depend on the candidate space; no untested improvement is inferred.
+
+The reported paired intervals are pointwise and unadjusted for multiple comparisons. Validation-selected-rule intervals are conditional on the selected cutoffs and omit cutoff-selection uncertainty. We add no Holm-adjusted inference or new climatology paired intervals here. The existing event results answer this comment without new training, figures or tables. Final page and line numbers will be added after full-manuscript assembly.}
+
+\begingroup
+\makeatletter
+\expandafter\let\csname r@fig:r2m4_event_csi\expandafter\endcsname\csname r@manuscript-fig:r2m4_event_csi\endcsname
+\makeatother
+\EvidenceBox{Existing fixed-rule results (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:event_detection}; final page and line numbers pending)}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Figure~\ref{fig:r2m4_event_csi} compares the two decision rules using saved single-run forecasts. Under the fixed rule, Persistence CSI was 0.139 at Q95, 0.076 at Q97.5, 0.166 at 0.5~mm\,h$^{-1}$ and 0.058 at 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$; the six learning models reached at most 0.071, 0.009, 0.113 and 0.011, respectively. All 24 learning-model differences at these four thresholds were negative, with each pointwise 95\% paired circular-block interval below zero; this is not a multiple-comparison-adjusted claim (Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_fixed_events}). For example, XGBoost minus Persistence was $-0.0681$ at Q95, with interval $[-0.1131,-0.0219]$, and $-0.0533$ at 0.5~mm\,h$^{-1}$, with interval $[-0.0949,-0.0118]$. At 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$, the corresponding difference was $-0.0472$ with interval $[-0.0791,-0.0194]$. Several zero CSI values occurred because the predicted means never reached the fixed threshold and no event forecast was issued, not because missing scores were replaced by zero.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing validation-selected results and AUC boundary (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:event_detection}; final page and line numbers pending)}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Validation-selected cutoffs changed the operating rule rather than removing the fixed-rule failure. At Q95, five learning models had higher CSI than the separately optimized Persistence forecast; the Transformer's difference was negative and its interval included zero. At 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$, four learning-model intervals supported higher CSI, while those for Transformer and Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention included zero. Learning-model FAR was approximately 0.73--0.80 at Q95 and 0.86--0.92 at 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Their AUCs were approximately 0.80--0.85 and 0.82--0.87, respectively, compared with Persistence values of 0.747 and 0.732. This ranking information does not establish an advantage in fixed-rule CSI. Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m4_tuned_events} reports the selected cutoffs, full scores and paired intervals. Confidence intervals are conditional on the selected decision thresholds and do not account for uncertainty in threshold selection.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Existing 1.0 mm h$^{-1}$ event diagnostic (Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:event_detection}; final page and line numbers pending)}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{At the fixed mean-based rule $c_\tau=\tau=1.0$~mm\,h$^{-1}$, XGBoost detected only one of the 91 test events, whereas the other learning models in the saved-prediction diagnostic detected none. Selecting $c_\tau$ on the validation set instead increased test POD to approximately 0.26--0.62 across these models, but FAR was approximately 0.86--0.92. Their test ROC AUC values were approximately 0.82--0.87, compared with 0.73 for persistence. Thus, the forecasts retained information for ranking exceedance events, even when their means rarely reached the event-intensity threshold. Lowering the decision cutoff recovered detections at the cost of many false alarms; it did not resolve the high-threshold event-prediction problem.}
+}
+\endgroup
 
